## 2. Percentage rollout with a stable hash
   concept: bucket = sha256(flag key + member id) mod 10000; on when bucket < percentage x 100. Raising the percentage only adds buckets, so a member who had the feature keeps it
   [server] NOTIFY flags_changed projection-v2: cached copy dropped
     1%: stable    95 on (0.95%), kept 0, lost 0 | naive    90 on, lost 0 | 10000 evaluations, 1 flag read from Postgres
   [server] NOTIFY flags_changed projection-v2: cached copy dropped
    10%: stable  1018 on (10.18%), kept 95, lost 0 | naive   980 on, lost 79 | 10000 evaluations, 1 flag read from Postgres
   [server] NOTIFY flags_changed projection-v2: cached copy dropped
    50%: stable  4972 on (49.72%), kept 1018, lost 0 | naive  5019 on, lost 496 | 10000 evaluations, 1 flag read from Postgres
   [server] NOTIFY flags_changed projection-v2: cached copy dropped
   100%: stable 10000 on (100.00%), kept 4972, lost 0 | naive 10000 on, lost 0 | 10000 evaluations, 1 flag read from Postgres
   ok: every step is within 3 standard deviations of its target
   ok: nobody lost the feature as the percentage grew from 1 to 10 to 50 to 100%
   ok: a fresh random draw per request takes the feature away from members at every step but the last
   at 10%, a second page load gave a different answer to 0 members (stable hash) and 1747 members (fresh random draw)
   ok: the stable hash gives a member the same answer on every request
   members in the first 10% of both projection-v2 and new-statement-page: 108 (independent flags: about 10% x 10% x 10000 = 100)
   ok: the flag key is in the hash, so each flag picks a different 10% of members
   [server] NOTIFY flags_changed projection-v2: cached copy dropped
   at 50%: the server and this process agree on 200 of 200 members
   ok: any process computes the same answer for the same member (no shared session state)

