## 4. Ops kill switch during an incident
   concept: the live valuation call is slow and the valuation service is timing out; on-call turns it off and the very next evaluation stops calling it
   [server] NOTIFY flags_changed ops-live-valuation: cached copy dropped
   before the switch: 19 requests, mean 42.9 ms, live valuation on 19
   after the commit:  881 requests, mean 0.9 ms, live valuation on 0 (requests that started after the commit)
   ok: the kill switch takes effect within one evaluation cycle (at most one request after the commit still made the call)
   ok: the statement gets fast again once the expensive call is off

## 5. Without LISTEN/NOTIFY, the cache TTL is the delay
   concept: two clients with the same 5 s TTL; only one LISTENs. The incident is resolved and the switch goes back on: who sees it when?
   [server] NOTIFY flags_changed ops-live-valuation: cached copy dropped
   LISTEN client saw the change after 9 ms, TTL-only client after 4704 ms
   ok: NOTIFY invalidates in milliseconds; the TTL alone takes seconds
   server cache: 4456 evaluations, 16 reads from Postgres, 10 invalidations by NOTIFY
   ok: the cache answers almost every evaluation locally

